    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214">

                <pb facs="0374" n="282" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg282"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/1776</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stovall</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-10T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 10, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2265. Your 3884, July 5, 9 p.m. It seems obvious that the German peace plan is to
                    sacrifice in the west in order to obtain a vast incalculable gain in the east.
                        Herron’s attention might be called to the fact that the
                    Eastern Question is not dealt with in the Bavarian tentative peace program and
                    told that a discussion of a settlement which did not put all the Russian,
                    Turkish, and Eastern cards on the table was not even remotely possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
